---
import Base from '../../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import {estrofasDeCanto} from '../../../../lib/corpus';
const selections=[1159,1160,1161];
const sections=[{"heading":"Why solidarity matters here","body":"Fierro has already told a story of coercion, loss and disconnection from his family. When he calls for his sons to remain together, the stakes are personal. Unity is not merely an abstract national motto; it answers a broken household."},{"heading":"The warning within the stanza","body":"The verse imagines brothers being vulnerable to outsiders if they turn against each other. It is an image of exposure, not an instruction to approve every action by a family member. Solidarity can coexist with honesty and disagreement."},{"heading":"Respect and moral reputation","body":"The neighboring stanzas connect unity with shame, self-respect and regard for elders. Placing the famous lines between other counsels shows how Fierro imagines personal conduct as a network of relationships."},{"heading":"What the line can mean today","body":"Families can read the stanza as a call to support one another in hard times, while also recognizing that healthy unity cannot require silence about harm. That final distinction is a modern ethical interpretation, not an extra verse attributed to the poem."}];
const stanzas=estrofasDeCanto('vuelta',32).filter(s=>selections.includes(s.numero));
---
<Base lang="en-US" adKind="article" pageType="Article" title="Los Hermanos Sean Unidos: The Meaning of Martín Fierro’s Famous Advice" description="Understand 'Los hermanos sean unidos' in its original Spanish stanza, with English commentary on sibling unity, family, solidarity and historical context.">
<article class="study"><nav class="crumb"><a href="/en/">English home</a> / <a href="/en/library/">Popular library</a> / <a href="/en/martin-fierro-advice-to-his-sons/">Advice to his sons</a></nav><header><p class="eyebrow">FATHERHOOD · FAMILY · WISDOM · ORIGINAL SPANISH VERSES</p><h1>Los Hermanos Sean Unidos: The Meaning of Martín Fierro’s Famous Advice</h1><p class="intro">Few lines from Martín Fierro are remembered as widely as the call for brothers to stay united. Read in context, this is more than a slogan about togetherness: it is an appeal spoken after painful family separation.</p></header><figure><img src="/media/fotografias-v3/consejos-fogon-hijos.webp" alt="Modern illustrated reconstruction of Martín Fierro giving counsel to three young men around a campfire"/><figcaption>Contemporary editorial illustration; not an archival document.</figcaption></figure><div class="reading"><section><h2>The original verses, Canto XXXII (1879)</h2><p>These selected stanzas are drawn directly from the site's original Spanish corpus; editorial explanation follows separately.</p>{stanzas.map(s=><section class="stanza" lang="es"><h3>Estrofa {s.numero}</h3><blockquote>{s.versos.map(v=><span>{v.texto}</span>)}</blockquote><a href={`/en/martin-fierro-advice-to-his-sons/#${s.id}`} lang="en">See the stanza in the complete canto</a></section>)}</section><AdSlot id="article-02-after-original-verses" kind="article"/><section><h2>Reading the poem with care</h2>{sections.map((s,i)=><><section><h3>{s.heading}</h3><p>{s.body}</p></section>{i===1&&<AdSlot id="article-03-mid-commentary" kind="article"/>}{i===2&&<AdSlot id="article-04-late-commentary" kind="article"/>}</>)}</section><section class="sources"><h2>Original text and reading companions</h2><p>José Hernández, <em>La vuelta de Martín Fierro</em> (1879), Canto XXXII. The quotations are read from the same repository corpus as the Spanish reader. This article is original English interpretation, not a translation or a claim about Hernández's personal beliefs.</p><p><a href="/leer/vuelta/32/" lang="es">Read the complete original canto</a> · <a href="/en/martin-fierro-advice-to-his-sons/">All 31 stanzas with commentary</a> · <a href="/en/library/themes-and-analysis/">Literary analysis</a></p></section><AdSlot id="article-05-end" kind="article"/></div></article>
